<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonhard Euler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1707">1707</a>–<a href="/years/1783">1783</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonhard Euler was a Swiss mathematician and physicist from Basel, one of the most prolific and influential mathematicians in history. Working at the academies of St Petersburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, he made fundamental contributions to analysis, number theory, mechanics and the beginnings of graph theory, and introduced much modern notation, including f(x), e and i. He continued working after losing his sight.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonhard-euler.jpg" alt="Leonhard Euler" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakob Emanuel Handmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonhard_Euler_-_Jakob_Emanuel_Handmann_(Kunstmuseum_Basel).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leonhard Euler was born in Basel on 15 April 1707, the oldest of four children of Paul III Euler, a pastor of the Reformed Church, and Marguerite, née Brucker. Soon after his birth the family moved to Riehen, where his father became pastor and Euler spent most of his childhood. His father, who had taken courses from Jacob Bernoulli at the University of Basel, taught him mathematics. Around the age of eight he went to live with his maternal grandmother and enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> school in Basel, and he was also tutored privately by the theologian Johannes Burckhardt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Daniel Bernoulli, who had entered the Imperial Russian Academy of Sciences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, recommended Euler for a post, and Euler arrived there in May 1727. He was soon promoted from the medical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> to the mathematics department, lodged with Bernoulli, mastered Russian and worked as a medic in the Russian Navy. In June 1741, concerned about turmoil in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, he left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-academy-of-sciences">Berlin Academy</a> at the invitation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> for 25 years. There he published the Introductio in analysin infinitorum in 1748 and the Institutiones calculi differentialis in 1755. In 1766 he accepted an invitation to return to Saint Petersburg after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> came to the throne.</p><p>Euler married Katharina Gsell on 7 January 1734; of their 13 children, five survived childhood. After her death in 1773 he married her half-sister, Salome Abigail Gsell. He became almost blind in his right eye in 1738, and a cataract in his left was discovered in 1766 and left him almost totally blind. With the help of scribes his productivity in many areas increased; in 1775 he produced on average one mathematical paper a week.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His writings, collected as the Opera Omnia, include 866 publications and over 4,500 letters. Euler introduced or popularised much modern notation, including f(x) for a function, e for the base of the natural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logarithm">logarithm</a>, the Greek letter sigma for summations and i for the imaginary unit, and he popularised the use of the Greek letter pi, which originated with the Welsh mathematician William Jones. In 1735 he solved the Basel problem, finding the sum of the reciprocals of the squares, posed by Pietro Mengoli in 1644.</p><p>His solution of the Seven Bridges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> problem, showing that no path crosses each bridge exactly once, is considered the first theorem of graph theory. He also found the formula relating the vertices, edges and faces of a convex polyhedron. Euler died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> on 18 September 1783 of a brain haemorrhage, after discussing the newly discovered planet Uranus with Anders Johan Lexell. He was buried beside Katharina at the Smolensk Lutheran Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leonhard Euler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…its university founded in 1460 and printers such as Johann Froben, became a centre of humanism where Erasmus lived and Hans Holbein the Younger worked, and in the eighteenth century the Bernoulli family, <b>Leonhard Euler</b> and Albrecht von Haller gave the Confederacy a leading place in European science.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Basel. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.56,7.59&amp;year=1756" aria-label="Basel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Leonhard Euler"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5437 6031h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5437 6031h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.56,7.59&amp;year=1756">What was here in 1756? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
